#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:1
msgid "Inspecting Enums"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:3
msgid ""
"We can easily inspect the contents of an enum using **pattern matching**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:8
msgid "\"We got an object: {obj:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:9
msgid "\"We got an array: {array:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:10
msgid "\"We got a string: {string:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:11
msgid "\"We got a number: {num}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:12
msgid "\"We got a bool: {b}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:13
msgid "\"We got a null\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:20
msgid ""
"Continuing with our JSON parsing example, we can easily determine which kind "
"of value we got by pattern matching on the resulting enum."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:23
msgid ""
"This makes enums a good fit for scenarios where we want to handle different "
"types at runtime, but want to retain type information and the ability to "
"directly inspect the concrete value."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/inspecting.md:27
msgid ""
"This is a big advantage enums have over `dyn`: With `dyn` we can't easily "
"downcast to the specific concrete type, and are generally restricted to "
"going through the trait interface. Later we'll see that we can support "
"downcasting with `dyn`, but doing so requires extra setup that isn't "
"necessary with enums."
msgstr ""
